# seroval < 1.4.1 rebuilds a serialized node tree in
# packages/seroval/src/core/context/deserializer.ts, and the names the
# payload supplies resolve straight into the deserializer's fixed record
# tables. The switch in `deserialize` returns `CONSTANT_VAL[node.s]` and
# `SYMBOL_REF[node.s]`, and `deserializeError` selects its constructor with
# `ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s]` before `new construct(deserializeString(node.m))`.
# No lookup checks that the name is the record's own property, so a key the
# table does not define still resolves through the record's prototype chain
# -- the advisory's "overriding constant value and error deserialization"
# (GHSA-3rxj-6cgf-8cfw, CWE-502), which is what hands back non-table values
# (a `constructor` key reaches the `Object` function) and with them indirect
# access to JS evaluation. The fixed revision, bundled in the 1.4.1/1.4.2
# release commit ce9408ebc87312fcad345a73c172212f2a798060, routes all three
# lookups through `deserializeKnownValue`, which returns `record[key]` only
# after `Object.hasOwn(record, key)` and otherwise raises
# `SerovalMalformedNodeError`.
#
# The same release hardens the property-write arm of the same file:
# `deserializeProperties` wrote `result[deserializeString(key)] = value` with
# no key validation at all; the fixed revision routes string keys through
# `isValidKey` (denying `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` and the
# dunder accessors) and stores denied names with `Object.defineProperty`.
# That arm is detected: on the vulnerable revision VYQL-INJ-013 reports the
# dynamic write at deserializer.ts:240 and :242 with `code.ExternalEntryInput`
# on the serialized fields as source, and on the patched revision the
# string-key write inside `assignStringProperty` is suppressed by the
# existing `definePropertyPrototypeKeyGuard` control binding. Tests (c) and
# (d) pin that live half.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked: vocabulary gap, for the arm the advisory names.
#
# 1. Nothing on the lookup path is judged, on either revision. Scanning the
#    real checkout with the full findings view, `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan
#    --cache off --max-ram 6GB`, three runs per revision, gives 88 findings
#    (75 at or above high) on the vulnerable revision and 84 on the patched
#    one, both stable across the three runs. The only rows that move across
#    the fix are the property-write arm: VYQL-INJ-013 on deserializer.ts:240
#    and :242 goes to a single residual row at :338, the symbol-keyed write
#    behind `isValidSymbol`. Everything else in the diff is the file being
#    rewritten (line shifts) and two scratch files the release deletes.
#    `vyql match` on the vulnerable deserializer.ts labels
#    `code.ExternalEntryInput` on the params at :560 and :637 and nothing
#    else in the 300-646 range apart from `code.SqlExecution` on
#    `ctx.base.refs.get(...)` arguments (:513, :525, :537, :574) -- the known
#    `.get` over-labelling, not this defect. The `ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s]`
#    lookups at :380/:384 and the `CONSTANT_VAL`/`SYMBOL_REF` returns at
#    :566/:604 carry no label of any kind. `code.ReflectionInvoke`
#    (VYQL-DESER-003) has a Java and a PHP emitter and none for JS/TS.
# 2. The taint engine has the path. `vyql graph` on the vulnerable file
#    shows deserializeError#param#node -> Attr(node.s) ->
#    Subscript(ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR.__subscript) -> Call#1370 path=construct at
#    deserializer.ts:384, so only the sink label is missing.
# 3. The binding vocabulary cannot express the join. Bisected with probes
#    against `deserializeError`'s `analysis.function.context` node (at :376):
#    present are `index_base:ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR`, `index_key:node.s`,
#    `subscript:ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s]`, `call_path:construct` and
#    `call:construct`; absent are `own_property_key_guard`,
#    `prototype_key_guard`, `prototype_name_guard` and -- the load-bearing
#    one -- any `assign:` token, because `const construct = ...` is a
#    `variable_declarator` and only `assignment_expression` emits `assign:`.
#    No clause can say "this invocation's callee is the local the computed
#    read populated", so the callee is reachable only by its own name, which
#    is the rejected layout-keyed form. `callExpr` bindings cannot take the
#    presence route instead: a `callExpr where node.context.language == ...`
#    clause fails to lower ("query predicate node.context.language is not
#    implemented in scanner IR lowering") while the run still exits 0 and
#    reports zero findings.
# 4. The invocation is the discriminating conjunct, and it is the one the
#    join would buy. serializer.ts:394 reads the same table shape,
#    `OBJECT_FLAG_CONSTRUCTOR[flag.type]`, but concatenates the retrieved
#    value into a string and never invokes it -- so a fact joining a computed
#    read to the invocation it feeds removes all four of this repository's
#    production false positives at a stroke. Measured across the sibling
#    checkouts under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ (87,926 JS/TS files): 2,669 files
#    contain a local assigned from a computed keyed read that is later
#    invoked, 787 of them non-minified source, 477 of those with no
#    `hasOwn`/`Object.create` anywhere in the file. A presence binding on
#    that shape reports hundreds of files of ordinary registry dispatch, so
#    the fact only pays as a taint-gated sink on the call node -- which needs
#    the callee-provenance fact *and* a route for a `callExpr` binding to
#    reach it.
# 5. The fixed revision's own vocabulary is not a discriminator. The patched
#    form's `deserializeKnownValue` and its `Object.hasOwn(record, key)` live
#    in the helper the caller now delegates to, so the caller's own context
#    never carries `own_property_key_guard`; what separates the revisions is
#    that the caller no longer performs an unguarded computed read at all.
#    That separation is semantic, not layout -- but it is only expressible
#    with the join of point 3/4.
#
# What the vocabulary would need, per ENGINE-GAPS.md: a callee-provenance
# fact for JavaScript call and `new` nodes -- the local or parameter whose
# value the invocation consumes -- emitted in a form a `callExpr` binding
# can read, so a sink binding can label an invocation whose callee is
# resolved by a computed key off a param-crossing record and let
# VYQL-DESER-003's taint rule do the attacker-controlled half. The taint
# path is there; only the sink identification is not expressible.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1725: (a) the deserializer resolves serialized names into constant and constructor tables with no own-property check"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-167
  reject VYQL-DESER-003
  code
  ```
  const CONSTANT_VAL: Record<string, unknown> = {};
  const ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR: Record<string, ErrorConstructor> = {};

  export function deserialize(node: any): unknown {
    switch (node.t) {
      case 0:
        return CONSTANT_VAL[node.s];
      case 6:
        return deserializeError(node);
      default:
        return undefined;
    }
  }

  function deserializeError(node: any): Error {
    const construct = ERROR_CONSTRUCTOR[node.s];
    return assignIndexedValue(node.i, new construct(node.m));
  }

  function assignIndexedValue(id: number, value: unknown): unknown {
    return value;
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (b) the patched deserializer resolves table names through an own-property check"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-167
  reject VYQL-DESER-003
  code
  ```
  const CONSTANT_VAL: Record<string, unknown> = {};

  function deserializeKnownValue<T>(node: unknown, record: Record<string, T>, key: string): T {
    if (Object.hasOwn(record, key)) {
      return record[key];
    }
    throw new MalformedNodeError(node);
  }

  export function deserialize(node: any): unknown {
    switch (node.t) {
      case 0:
        return deserializeKnownValue(node, CONSTANT_VAL, node.s);
      default:
        return undefined;
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (c) the unvalidated property write keyed by the serialized record is reported"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-013
  code
  ```
  function deserializeString(current: string): string {
    return current;
  }

  export function deserializeProperties(record: any): Record<string, unknown> {
    const result: Record<string, unknown> = {};
    const len = record.s;
    const keys = record.k;
    const vals = record.v;
    for (let i = 0, key: unknown; i < len; i++) {
      key = keys[i];
      if (typeof key === 'string') {
        result[deserializeString(key)] = vals[i];
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (d) the key-checked write with a defineProperty fallback is not reported"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file deserializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-013
  code
  ```
  function isValidKey(key: string): boolean {
    switch (key) {
      case 'constructor':
      case '__proto__':
      case 'prototype':
        return false;
      default:
        return true;
    }
  }

  function assignStringProperty(
    object: Record<string, unknown>,
    key: string,
    value: unknown,
  ): void {
    if (isValidKey(key)) {
      object[key] = value;
    } else {
      Object.defineProperty(object, key, {
        value,
        configurable: true,
        enumerable: true,
        writable: true,
      });
    }
  }

  function assignProperty(
    object: Record<string, unknown>,
    key: unknown,
    value: unknown,
  ): void {
    if (typeof key === 'string') {
      assignStringProperty(object, key, value);
    }
  }

  export function deserializeProperties(record: any): Record<string, unknown> {
    const result: Record<string, unknown> = {};
    const keys = record.k;
    const vals = record.v;
    for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
      assignProperty(result, keys[i], vals[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```

test "rank1725: (e) registry dispatch that reads a computed key but never invokes the retrieved value is not reported"
  lang javascript
  profile library
  file serializer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-167
  reject VYQL-DESER-003
  code
  ```
  const OBJECT_FLAG_CONSTRUCTOR: Record<string, string | undefined> = {};

  export function resolveFlags(flags: Array<{ type: string; value: string }>): string | undefined {
    let result = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < flags.length; i++) {
      const flag = flags[i];
      result += OBJECT_FLAG_CONSTRUCTOR[flag.type] + '(' + flag.value + '),';
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```
